+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | In Progress          |
+
+Every registered tool declares an `inputSchema` in `src/types/ai.ts`, and `ToolRegistry.execute` in `src/orchestration/tools/registry.ts` validates the incoming `input` against that JSON Schema before calling the tool's executor. Because a malformed argument object is a model-call mistake that the model can self-correct on its next tool round in `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts`, schema rejection returns a structured `ToolResponse` describing the validation failure rather than throwing out of the turn; `execute` throws only when the named tool is not registered.
+
+## Schema compilation and per-call resolution
+
+- A static `ToolDefinition` has its `inputSchema` compiled at `register` time, and an uncompilable schema throws an `Error` naming the tool before the server accepts traffic ([validated by: throws at register time when a static tool schema cannot be compiled, naming the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L116)).
+- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` is resolved on each `execute` call and validated against the `inputSchema` returned for that call ([validated by: validates a function-valued tool definition against the schema resolved for each call](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L131)).
+- When a function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` returns an `inputSchema` that cannot be compiled at `execute` time, `execute` returns a rejection `ToolResponse` and logs a `WARN` rather than throwing out of the turn.
+
+## Validation outcomes and executor pass-through
+
+- When `input` omits a property listed in `inputSchema.required`, `execute` does not call the executor and returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the missing property as required and leaves `clientMessages` and `suppressAssistantResponse` unset ([validated by: rejects input missing a required property with a ToolResponse naming the property and without invoking the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L47)).
+- When `input` supplies a wrong-typed property or a value outside an `enum`, `execute` does not call the executor, returns a `ToolResponse` naming the field path and expected type or constraint, logs a `WARN` under category `'tool'` with the tool name and error count, and includes no property values from `input` in either `result` or the log line ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed property naming the field path and expected type without leaking any input value in the result or logs](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L72)).
+- When `inputSchema` sets `additionalProperties: false`, an unlisted property is rejected without calling the executor, and the rejection names the unexpected key.
+- When `inputSchema` does not set `additionalProperties: false` — including the minimal `{type: 'object'}` schema — unlisted properties are allowed and `input` is handed to the executor unchanged without stripping extra keys, coercing types, or injecting defaults.
+- Calling `execute` with an unknown tool name throws `Error('Unknown tool: <name>')` rather than returning a `ToolResponse`.
+
+## Orchestrator tool loop recovery
+
+- Inside `createChatOrchestrator`, a schema-rejected tool call feeds its rejection `ToolResponse.result` back to the provider as a `tool_result` message so the model can retry with valid arguments and complete the turn, while passing the valid retry payload to the executor unchanged ([validated by: returns a schema rejection to the model in the orchestrator tool loop and forwards valid input unchanged on retry](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L172)).
